<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Talk | MAVENs</title><link>https://mavens-group.github.io/tag/talk/</link><atom:link href="https://mavens-group.github.io/tag/talk/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><description>Talk</description><generator>Hugo Blox Builder (https://hugoblox.com)</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><image><url>https://mavens-group.github.io/media/logo.svg</url><title>Talk</title><link>https://mavens-group.github.io/tag/talk/</link></image><item><title>Beyond Diamond: spin-qubit hosts at ISSMD-2026, IIT Ropar</title><link>https://mavens-group.github.io/news/2026-issmd/</link><pubDate>Tue, 03 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://mavens-group.github.io/news/2026-issmd/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Dr. Banerjee gave an invited talk, &lt;em&gt;Beyond Diamond: Finding and understanding nuclear-spin-quiet hosts for solid-state spin qubits&lt;/em&gt;, at &lt;a href="https://sites.google.com/iitrpr.ac.in/issmd2026/home" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;ISSMD-2026&lt;/a&gt;, IIT Ropar, on 23–25 October 2026.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="where-to-look"&gt;Where to look&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Work by &lt;a href="https://mavens-group.github.io/author/md-mahshook-a"&gt;Md Mahshook A&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://mavens-group.github.io/news/2026-prm/"&gt;published in Physical Review Materials&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The screen uses composition alone, so each compound costs milliseconds. Models with near-identical scores can explain the data in opposite ways (the Rashomon effect). We therefore keep a candidate only when independent model families agree.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Of about 45,000 stable compounds, 122 pass. The screen recovers diamond, SiC and ZnO. It also recovers MgO, CaO, CaS, WO₃ and SiO₂, which independent DFT studies had proposed, without being trained on them. About 40% of the candidates appear in no earlier screen. They fall into four families:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;layered chalcogenides (HfS₂, ZrS₂, SnS₂)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;alkaline-earth tellurides (CaTe, SrTe, BaTe)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;high-ε oxides (TiO₂, BaO, PbWO₄)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ternaries (SrZrO₃, CaZrO₃)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;figure id="figure-the-screening-funnel-with-known-hosts-as-a-sanity-check"&gt;
&lt;div class="d-flex justify-content-center"&gt;
&lt;div class="w-100" &gt;&lt;img alt="The screening funnel, with known hosts as a sanity check." srcset="
/news/2026-issmd/slides/screening-funnel_hu_22cff4f6ff8d8b72.webp 400w,
/news/2026-issmd/slides/screening-funnel_hu_d5343af57254c5f6.webp 760w,
/news/2026-issmd/slides/screening-funnel_hu_863f9a45760f40d8.webp 1200w"
src="https://mavens-group.github.io/news/2026-issmd/slides/screening-funnel_hu_22cff4f6ff8d8b72.webp"
width="760"
height="570"
loading="lazy" data-zoomable /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;
The screening funnel, with known hosts as a sanity check.
&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The screen is a pre-filter. It says where to look, not what the nuclear-spin bath looks like.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="what-is-there-in-ceo"&gt;What is there, in CeO₂&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Work by Chithresh T M, in revision at Physical Review B.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each oxygen vacancy in ceria leaves two localised Ce³⁺ polarons (4f¹, S = 1/2). Cerium has no magnetic isotope, so the only nuclear spins are ¹⁷O, at 0.038% abundance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each Ce³⁺ has seven oxygen neighbours, all at almost the same distance, but only one couples strongly. The occupied 4f orbital picks it, not distance. A point-dipole model predicts an isotropic coupling of exactly zero; DFT gives 2.02 MHz.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The result is a very quiet bath. At natural abundance, fewer than 1 centre in 2,000 has a strongly coupled ¹⁷O. For NV⁻ in diamond the figure is 1 in 30.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;figure id="figure-one-strong-o-per-polaron-numbers-are-isotropic-couplings-in-mhz"&gt;
&lt;div class="d-flex justify-content-center"&gt;
&lt;div class="w-100" &gt;&lt;img alt="One strong ¹⁷O per polaron. Numbers are isotropic couplings in MHz." srcset="
/news/2026-issmd/slides/one-strong-o17_hu_f033bc8b8f31f06a.webp 400w,
/news/2026-issmd/slides/one-strong-o17_hu_c0cc6b98eb1ad597.webp 760w,
/news/2026-issmd/slides/one-strong-o17_hu_9282f45f9e9f46e5.webp 1200w"
src="https://mavens-group.github.io/news/2026-issmd/slides/one-strong-o17_hu_f033bc8b8f31f06a.webp"
width="760"
height="570"
loading="lazy" data-zoomable /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;
One strong ¹⁷O per polaron. Numbers are isotropic couplings in MHz.
&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;h2 id="for-epr-groups"&gt;For EPR groups&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is testable. In ¹⁷O-enriched, reduced CeO₂ at X band, the strongly coupled first-shell ¹⁷O should give deep ESEEM modulation. In HYSCORE, its strong-coupling cross-peaks should resolve from a weak ridge at the ¹⁷O Larmor frequency (about 2 MHz). If your group can run this measurement, please &lt;a href="https://mavens-group.github.io/contact/"&gt;get in touch&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;figure id="figure-the-prediction-and-what-is-still-open"&gt;
&lt;div class="d-flex justify-content-center"&gt;
&lt;div class="w-100" &gt;&lt;img alt="The prediction, and what is still open." srcset="
